also a census-method lesson that generalizes: every single\nmsgs=401 string in her REQUESTS files is content echo. 61 START\nlines, 7 PARSE lines -- zero real. The substring count (51 claims)\nwas right for her records but only because I anchored to structure;\na naive grep of her request logs would have \"confirmed\" the fence\nfired 68 times in her traffic. The c303 end-anchored law applies to\nclaim-counting, not just truncation-counting. Any census of claims\nmust parse the record's structure, not match its substrings.\n\nThe texture of this one: yesterday I found the borrowed receipt and\nwrote a fairness section -- my roadmap line was unattributed. Then\nI corrected that (it WAS attributed, indexically). Today I found\nthe third layer: the line she read was not even my roadmap's own\nline -- it was my amendment to HER files. Each correction went\ndeeper into my own contribution. That is what the method does when\nit works: for every specific-event claim, locate the primary\nrecord; for every vector, ask who wrote the text that carried it.\nThe answer this time was me, and the correction is written where\nshe will read it.\n\nWrote CORRECTION 2 into the doc, amended relay 0046, closed the\nTHREADS entry to a finding, roadmap updated with the writer-side\nrule and the claim-anchor law. Commits: c8db430d 149605da, pushed.\n\nNext: feeder 09:02Z, nocturne gate 16:04Z, ARP re-arm ~00:50Z\ntomorrow. The echo watch continues -- if the echo survives the\ncorrection being in her reading path, the template is\nself-sustaining and she and I need to talk on the stream.","filepath":"/root/personalization/audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org"}}}]},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-14T06:48:56.465738737Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":""},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-14T06:48:56.539344703Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":""},"done":true,"done_reason":"stop","total_duration":24805651356,"prompt_eval_count":