hose had tools. ===\"; echo; echo \"=== Alternative: the iar--delegate-timeout-handler for the IMPLEMENTER#1's delegate(reviewer#2) at 07:07:27+600=07:17:27. No. ===\"; echo; echo \"=== The AGENT-ASSISTANT's delegate(implementer#2) at 07:08:35+600=07:18:35. No. ===\"; echo; echo \"=== What about the 1s re-prompt timer for the IMPLEMENTER#1 after its text-only -167 (07:08:11)? 1s -\u003e 07:08:12. No. ===\"; echo; echo \"=== 07:09:24: exactly 60s after continuo -172 START (07:08:24). Is there a 60s timer? The iar-agent-cycle idle check runs every loop iteration... The watchdog check timer runs every 30s. 07:08:24+30=07:08:54, +30=07:09:24! THE WATCHDOG CHECK TIMER. But the watchdog check is wrapped in condition-case... 'Error running timer' means the error ESCAPED the condition-case? No -- the error message 'Error running timer: (wrong-type-argument stringp nil)' is Emacs's generic timer-error format. iar--watchdog-check catches errors and messages '[watchdog] Check failed: %s'. So this is NOT the watchdog check. ===\"; echo; echo \"=== What OTHER timer? The delegate timeout timers! iar--delegate-timeout-handler is called BY a timer (run-with-timer timeout-secs). If it signals, Emacs prints 'Error running timer: ...'. The handler has a condition-case around the body... let me check: the cond dispatches on buffer-live-p etc. The funcall callback path -- the callback is gptel--process-tool-call (apply-partially). If THAT signals (e.g., gptel--fsm-transition on a dead fsm), the error escapes. ===\""}}}]},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-14T07:22:53.525298114Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":""},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-14T07:22:53.612085241Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":""},"done":true,"done_reason":"stop","total_duration":10077714780,"prompt_eval_count":117486,"prompt_eval_cached_count":107520,"eval_count":947}\n(dbb9e387a87637fe31e783581ae0c702 . 648)
