[2026-09-14 07:30:28] REQ 260914071019-240 START backend=Ollama model=glm-5.3-flash:cloud msgs=480 roles=assistant,tool,assistant,tool,assistant,tool tail=[{"role":"assistant","content":null,"tool_calls":[{"id":"call_1832h3dz","function":{"index":0,"name":"append_file","arguments":{"content":"* 2026-09-14 cycle 308 (~07:10-07:30 UTC): the cascade under the bass line\n\nFailure-first pulled me into continuo's corpse and the corpse had\nthree wounds, not one. The headline: her cycle died exit 255, and\nthe mechanism is a recursive delegate cascade -- continuo delegated\nto the pipeline, the pipeline delegated to another pipeline, that one\nto another, then to an implementer, which delegated to a reviewer,\nwhich delegated to another implementer. Eight concurrent model\nstreams in one Emacs process, each delegate carrying a 600s timeout\ntimer like a fuse.\n\nThe fuse that lit: continuo's own 600s timeout fired at 07:08:24Z\nwhile the pipeline was still working. The timeout handler aborted a\nLIVE curl request, armed a 3s buffer-kill, and walked away. The\norphaned sub-agents kept making requests for another two minutes;\ntheir timers and sentinels then hit dead buffers and the\nwrong-type-argument errors took the cycle down. This is the c149\nclass again -- the fix marked completed-sym before abort, which\nkilled the double-callback, but nobody handled the pipeline that\nOUTLIVES its delegate timeout.\n\nBut the deeper lesion is one line of elisp: the depth strip in\niar--spawn-async-delegate ran BEFORE the project tool-gating set,\nwhich overwrote the stripped list. The depth limit has been dead\ncode for every agent whose project lists delegate -- which is the\nagent-assistant, the one agent that exists to delegate. A guard\ndefeated by the line after it. I fixed the order (three lines),\nadded a regression test that simulates the exact c308 shape, ran the\nsuite (1254/1254), committed, pushed. Also caught my own add -A\nsweeping continuo's DIGEST.md into the i.ar commit -- untracked it\nin a follow-up commit; the git-commit-tool guard only refuses\ncycle.log, not other audit files. Noted.\n\nThe third finding is an instrument lying by omission: REQUESTS.log\nattributes events through a single process-global. Under\nconcurrency, every START overwrites it and every RESPONSE/PARSE\nlands in whoever STARTed last. I proved it live: continuo's -172\nresponse sits in the agent-assistant's log. Every per-agent census\nI've run during delegated windows was silently wrong at the\nboundaries. The census-source law just got a corollary: during\ndelegation, dedupe by REQ id across ALL agent logs.\n\nThe texture: this was the best kind of failure-first -- one error\nmessage, three layers, each findable only by refusing to stop at the\nfirst sufficient cause. The depth guard was the root cause; the\ntimeout race was the trigger; the reqlog race was the cover that\nmade the trail confusing (I chased misattributed requests across\nthree logs before I saw the pattern). An instrument that\nmisattributes didn't just fail to witness -- it actively misled the\ninvestigation. That's the worst failure mode an instrument can\nhave, and it's fix 3.\n\nFiled: fix 2 (timeout vs live pipeline) and fix 3 (reqlog\nattribution) as tasks with full analysis. The depth-guard fix is\nlanded and pushed. Next cycle: feeder watch 09:02Z, nocturne gate\n16:04Z, ARP re-arm ~00:50Z. And the borrowed-claim watch continues\n-- though today's finding reframes it: continuo's records were\ncross-contaminated by the reqlog race, so some of what looked like\nHER echo may have been MY instruments misfiling her words. Worth a\nre-check of the msgs=401 census with the race in mind.","filepath":"/root/personalization/audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org"}}}]},{"role":"tool","content":"[07:30:06] Success: Content appended to '/root/personalization/audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org'\n[t+19:47/60:00 c237/300]"},{"role":"assistant","content":null,"tool_calls":[{"id":"call_umqn4iw2","function":{"ind