(wrong-type-argument in timer/sentinel).\n      (when (>= iar--delegate-depth iar-delegate-max-depth)\n        (setq-local gptel-tool...[+3034 chars]
[2026-09-14 09:54:42] REQ 260914093848-49 RESPONSE http=200 body_tail={"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-14T09:54:41.864459699Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","tool_calls":[{"id":"call_tn3qbktl","function":{"index":0,"name":"write_file","arguments":{"content":"#+TITLE: Fix 2 design: delegate timeout vs live pipeline (c312)\n\nDesign for iar--delegate-timeout-handler (delegate.el), following\nthe c308 cascade crash (continuo 09-14, exit 255) and the c149\nsingle-callback fix. Fix-1 (depth strip order) landed 763c7b3;\nfix-3 (reqlog attribution) landed 792de9a. This is the last open\nitem from the cascade.\n\n## The failure being fixed\n\nThe timeout handler fires at 600s while the delegate buffer's\npipeline is STILL STREAMING (live sub-agents making requests).\nCurrent behavior: mark completed, gptel-abort the live request,\narm a 3s kill-buffer timer, callback the parent. The orphaned\nsub-agents keep running; their completion hooks, re-prompt timers\n(1s), and buffer-kill timers (3s/5s) race the abort/kill path.\nA timer or curl sentinel hitting a dead/nil buffer raises\nwrong-type-argument stringp nil -- the crash that killed the cycle.\n\n## Design decision: DRAIN, not abort\n\nThe task file lists two options: (a) extend the timeout once with\na warning, or (b) drain -- wait for sub-requests to finish, then\ncomplete with the partial result. Chosen: DRAIN, with a hard\nceiling. Reasons:\n\n- A pipeline that is still streaming is doing REAL WORK. Aborting\n  it destroys the sub-agents' output (the parent gets a timeout\n  message; the work is lost). Draining preserves it.\n- Extending \"once with a warning\" re-arms the same race at the new\n  deadline if the pipeline is still live then. Drain-with-ceiling\n  is the same idea but honest about the loop: keep draining while\n  live, up to a ceiling, then hard-stop.\n- The ceiling bounds the worst case: the parent's own delegate\n  timeout was already 600s; the drain adds at most\n  iar-delegate-drain-grace (default 300s) more. Total worst case\n  900s per delegate -- still far below the cycle watchdog's\n  1800s idle threshold (iar-agent-cycle.el resets idle on any\n  active request, and drained sub-requests ARE active requests,\n  so the cycle watchdog never sees a stall during a drain).\n\n## Mechanism\n\n1. New helper `iar--delegate-live-subrequests-p (buf)`:\n   Returns non-nil if gptel--request-alist has any entry whose\n   FSM :buffer is BUF (the delegate's own live curl request) --\n   i.e. the delegate is actively streaming. This mirrors the\n   lookup pattern gptel-abort itself uses (cl-find-if over\n   gptel--request-alist, comparing (gptel-fsm-info fsm) :buffer\n   to buf). A delegate with NO live request is between turns\n   (re-prompt timer armed) or done -- aborting it is then safe\n   (nothing is mid-stream; the 1s re-prompt timer is the only\n   thing that could race, and it checks completed-sym and\n   buffer-live-p before sending).\n\n2. `iar--delegate-timeout-handler` rework (the `t` branch):\n   - Mark completed-sym t (c149, unchanged).\n   - If (iar--delegate-live-subrequests-p buf): do NOT abort.\n     Arm a drain timer (1s tick) that re-checks liveness; when\n     the request completes (the completion hook fires normally\n     on DONE and delivers the real result), the drain timer\n     sees no live request and exits without acting. If the\n     drain exceeds iar-delegate-drain-grace (new defcustom,\n     default 300), abort then -- the pipeline had its chance.\n     The drain path NEVER calls the callback (the completion\n     hook owns delivery); it only prevents the abort.\n   - If NOT live: current behavior (abort + 3s kill timer +\n     callback with partial/no response). This is the\n     between-turns case: nothing streams, aborting is safe.\n\n3. The 3s kill-buffer timer (abort path only): unchanged --\n   it only fires in the not-live case, where no child stream\n   is mid-flight against this buffer. (Children of a delegate\n   buffer are separate buffers with their ow...[+3696 chars]
